{
  "id": "ar_practical_ma",
  "title": "Arabic — practical Maghreb (French-based) romanization",
  "language": "ar",
  "script": "Arab",
  "kind": "practical",
  "status": "informal",
  "authority": "French-based conventions used in Algeria, Morocco and Tunisia (cf. IGN 1973 'Beirut amended, variant B'); not a formal standard",
  "description": "French-influenced ASCII spellings used in North Africa: ou for و and ū (Oualid, Taoufik, Mouloud), ch for ش (Rachid), dj for ج (Djamal), k for ق (Abdelkader), ss between vowels (Yassine), final -ne after a long vowel (Othmane), article el-, Abd el-X written solid (Abdelkader, Abderrahmane, Abdellah) and X ed-Din as Xeddine (Noureddine).",
  "sources": [
    "https://www.eki.ee/wgrs/rom1_ar.pdf", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Romanization_of_Arabic",
    "https://www.cjk.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/12/xofacv.pdf"
  ],
  "notes": [
    "IGN 1973 equivalences used: u/ū = ou (short u is written o: Omar, Othmane, Mostafa), sh = ch, j = dj, w = ou, s = ss between vowels, n = ne word-finally (here only after ā/ī), ’ not romanized, ʿayn dropped.",
    "ق is written k as in most Maghreb civil-status spellings (Abdelkader, Belkacem); IGN gives q/g/gu.",
    "Kasra before a word-final consonant is written e (Khaled, Abdelkader, Nasser); other colloquial vowel changes (Youssef, Mohamed, Driss) are lexical.",
    "Moroccan and Tunisian spellings often use j for ج (Jamal); dj is the Algerian form requested here.",
    "Output is ASCII from the rules, but the lexicon may supply French forms with diacritics (Aïcha, Fayçal), so the scheme is not flagged ascii."
  ],
  "alphabet": "ءآأؤإئابةتثجحخدذرزسشصضطظعغفقكلمنهوىيٱپچژگڤ",
  "hook": "arabic",
